concerns about  climate change and the impact of so many  people on the planet now  has led anna hughes to a life-changing  decision  she lives close to nature on a houseboat  in north london  but says she'll never have children  there are lots of people  on the planet and and there's only a  certain amount of space and a certain  number of resources that we have here so  um that that kind of comes to a crunch  point at some point doesn't it you can't  just  continue to have ever more people it's  just there's only  it doesn't work so i am i became quite  aware of that and i thought  actually that's not something i want to  contribute to  the current global population is nearly  8 billion  new predictions suggest the number will  peak in 2064  at nearly 10 billion but the figure  would then decline to around  8.8 billion by 2100  of the fastest shrinking populations  will be in asia  and europe populations are expected to  more than  halve in spain italy and japan  and it's predicted there will be some  big population  booms the population of sub-saharan  africa  could triple by 2100 to over 3 billion  it's easy to associate the world's  growing population with the climate  emergency  but some argue stabilizing the  population  is not the solution a lot of people link  population to climate change the problem  here is  actually not everybody emits the same  amount  so the interesting thing is it's not the  number of people that  causes climate change it's what we do  and how much we consume  and of course how much we admit  within 50 years we may see the global  population start to decline  by then without drastic action the full  consequences of our climate emergency  will also be known lisa holland sky news
 

